@@ -0,0 +1,202 @@
+# Persistent repacked-weight cache (GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE)
+
+## Why
+
+With the Q3_K repack patch, ggml-cpu repacks every repackable weight when the model loads (in the
+CPU_REPACK buffer type's `set_tensor`, single-threaded), and the repacked weights live in anonymous
+memory. For Ornith (16.2 GiB GGUF, 90 Q3_K expert tensors of about 9.67 GiB) load time went from
+9.6 s to 24.8 s, and the 9.7 GiB of repacked weights cannot be reclaimed by the kernel.
+
+The cache stores the repacked bytes on disk once and maps them on later loads, so a warm load skips
+the repack, and the weights become clean, file-backed pages that the kernel can drop and re-read.
+
+## Usage
+
+```
+export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE=$HOME/.cache/local-ai/repack   # unset or empty: exact old behaviour
+export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_VERIFY=1                       # default; 0 skips the payload hash on hits
+```
+
+The directory is created when needed. It works for every repacked type (Q4_0, Q4_K, Q5_K, Q6_K,
+Q3_K, IQ4_NL, MXFP4, Q8_0, Q1_0, and the x86/RISC-V variants), because it sits in the buffer type,
+not in a per-type repack function. POSIX only (Linux tested); on Windows the variable is ignored.
+
+Inspect and clean it with `tools/repack_cache.py`:
+
+```
+tools/repack_cache.py list  [--dir D] [-v] [--json]
+tools/repack_cache.py prune [--dir D] [--older-than DAYS] [--build B] [--patch P] [--keep-latest] [--dry-run]
+tools/repack_cache.py clear [--dir D] --yes
+```
+
+`list` groups entries by model, build and patch, with size, last use and status. `prune` always removes
+corrupt or truncated entries, entries whose model file is gone or changed (size, mtime or inode), and
+abandoned temp files older than 1 h. On request it also removes other builds or patches
+(`--build`, `--patch`), entries not used for N days (`--older-than`), and older versions of the same
+tensor (`--keep-latest`). "Last use" is the file mtime, which ggml updates on every warm hit.
+
+## Design
+
+### Integration point
+
+Everything is in ggml-cpu (`ggml/src/ggml-cpu/repack-cache.{h,cpp}` plus about 30 lines in `repack.cpp`).
+llama.cpp is unchanged. The loader already passes each repacked tensor to
+`ggml_backend_tensor_set(cur, <pointer into the GGUF mmap>, 0, nbytes)`, and that call reaches
+`ggml_backend_cpu_repack_buffer_set_tensor`. That is the single place where every repacked tensor goes
+by, with its name, type, shape, source bytes and destination.
+
+When `GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE` is set:
+
+1. `get_alignment` of the CPU_REPACK buffer type returns the page size instead of 64, so each
+   repacked tensor starts on a page boundary (at most one page of padding per tensor, about 1 MiB in total).
+2. `alloc_buffer` reserves the buffer with an anonymous `mmap` (page-aligned, owned by the cache),
+   wrapped with `ggml_backend_cpu_buffer_from_ptr`, and frees it with `munmap`.
+3. `set_tensor` calls `ggml_repack_cache_set_tensor`:
+   - **hit**: validate the header, then `mmap(tensor->data, len, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_FIXED, fd, header_size)`.
+     This replaces the tensor's anonymous pages with a private file mapping of the payload. `tensor->data` does not change,
+     so ggml-alloc, the scheduler and the kernels see the same tensor as before. Pages are faulted in from the page cache
+     on demand, and while clean they are reclaimable. `MAP_PRIVATE` means a stray write would be copy-on-write and could
+     never corrupt the cache file.
+   - **miss**: run the normal repack into the tensor, write the file, then map the newly written inode over the tensor in the
+     same way, so the cold load also ends up with file-backed weights and the anonymous copy is released.
+
+Per-tensor aliasing was simple enough to do in place, so the alternative "one file per model plus a separate
+`buffer_from_ptr` buffer" was not needed. That alternative would have needed llama.cpp changes to place the repacked
+tensors into a buffer created from the mapping. A tensor that cannot be mapped (not page-aligned, which happens only if
+the env var changes between `get_alignment` and `alloc_buffer`) is filled by `pread` from the cache file instead: the
+repack is still skipped, but the memory is anonymous.
+
+### File layout
+
+```
+<dir>/<model-basename>-<model-id12>/<tensor-name>.<keyhash16>.rpk
+<dir>/content/<tensor-name>.<keyhash16>.rpk          # no file identity (e.g. --no-mmap)
+```
+
+Each file is a header of one page (max(4096, page size) bytes) followed by the payload, which is exactly
+`ggml_nbytes(tensor)` repacked bytes and starts at a page-aligned offset, as `mmap` requires.
+
+| offset | field |
+|---|---|
+| 0 | magic `GGMLRPC1` |
+| 8 | u32 format version (1), u32 header size |
+| 16 | u64 payload size |
+| 24 | u64 xxh64 of the payload |
+| 32 | u64 xxh64 of the key text |
+| 40 | u64 creation time (ns) |
+| 48 | u32 key length, u32 reserved |
+| 56 | full key text (`name=value` lines) |
+| header_size-8 | u64 xxh64 of header bytes [0, header_size-8) |
+
+### Key
+
+The full key text is stored in the header and compared byte for byte on every hit. The file name only
+carries its hash. The key contains:
+
+- `format`: cache format version
+- `build`: `ggml_version()/ggml_commit()` (the commit ggml was configured from, with `-dirty` when relevant)
+- `patch`: the compile-time `GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_PATCH_ID`
+  (default `q3k-repack-1c148ab+mmid-gemm-0a3a679+repack-cache-v1`; override with `-DGGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_PATCH_ID=...`)
+- `cpu`: feature set (neon, dotprod, i8mm, sve, sme, fp16va, avx*, amx, riscv_v, vxe, sve_cnt)
+- `repack`: the selected repack layout (block size, interleave, column count, activation type), e.g. `blk110:i8:c8:q8_K`
+- `layout`: the xxh64 of that repack function's output for a fixed synthetic 2048x16 input, computed
+  once per process and per layout. If anyone changes a repack function's byte layout, even without
+  bumping the patch id or committing, the key changes. This guards against a dirty dev build reusing an old layout.
+- `tensor`, `type`, `ne`, `nb`, `nbytes`
+- source identity, one of:
+  - `src=file` (normal mmap load): the GGUF that backs the source pointer, found through `/proc/self/maps`
+    and confirmed with `stat` (device, inode): `model` path, `model_dev`, `model_ino`, `model_size`,
+    `model_mtime_ns`, `model_head` (xxh64 of the first 8 MiB of the file, which covers the GGUF header,
+    the KV metadata and usually the tensor infos; computed once per file), `tensor_offset`, and `src_sample`
+    (xxh64 of 8 x 1 KiB samples of the source tensor bytes)
+  - `src=content` (no file mapping, e.g. `--no-mmap`, where the bytes are already in RAM): `src_hash`, the xxh64 of all source bytes
+
+### Fallback rules
+
+Any problem falls back to the normal in-memory repack, so the load always produces correct weights, and
+the cache never aborts the process. Every error prints one machine-parseable line on stderr
+(capped at 32 per process):
+
+```
+ggml-repack-cache: ERROR <reason> key=<keyhash16> path=<file> [errno=N] [tensor=<name>]
+```
+
+| reason | meaning / action |
+|---|---|
+| `truncated` | file size != header + payload: repack, rewrite |
+| `bad-magic`, `bad-version`, `corrupt-header` | header damaged or from another format: repack, rewrite |
+| `size-mismatch`, `key-mismatch` | header valid but for another tensor (hash collision or a file copied over): repack, rewrite |
+| `corrupt-payload` | payload xxh64 differs (only checked with VERIFY=1, the default): pages restored to anonymous, repack, rewrite |
+| `read-failed`, `stat-failed`, `open-failed` | I/O error: repack, rewrite attempted |
+| `mmap-failed` | the file could not be mapped: data read with `pread` instead (cold path: kept in memory) |
+| `mkdir-failed`, `create-failed`, `write-failed`, `rename-failed` | could not store: weights stay in memory |
+| `disk-full` | ENOSPC/EDQUOT: weights stay in memory, no further writes in this process |
+| `alloc-failed` | could not reserve the mmap buffer: the plain buffer type is used (no cache) |
+
+Expected events are INFO, not errors:
+
+```
+ggml-repack-cache: INFO miss key=<keyhash16> path=<file> tensor=<name>        # cold cache, one per tensor
+ggml-repack-cache: INFO hit key=<keyhash16> path=<file> msg="..."             # first hit in the process
+ggml-repack-cache: INFO summary key=- path=<dir> hits=H misses=M written=W errors=E   # when a buffer is freed
+```
+
+A different model file, build, patch, CPU or layout produces a different key and so a different file name.
+That is a plain miss, never a reuse of stale data. Per-tensor details (`repack-cache: hit|miss|repack|write|error`)
+go to the ggml log at DEBUG level.
+
+### Atomic writes and concurrency
+
+A writer creates `.<name>.rpk.tmp.<pid>.<n>` with `O_EXCL`, writes the header and payload, `fsync`s, and
+`rename`s it over the final name, then `fsync`s the directory. Readers only ever see complete files. Two
+processes loading at the same time both write their own temp file, and the last rename wins. The content
+is identical, and each process maps the inode it wrote itself, not the name, so neither is affected by the
+other. Files are never modified in place: a corrupt entry is replaced by rename, and processes that still map
+the old inode keep a valid mapping. Leftover temp files (after a crash) are ignored by ggml and removed by
+`repack_cache.py prune` after one hour.
+
+Do not truncate or edit a cache file in place while a process maps it: shrinking a mapped file causes
+SIGBUS in that process. `rm`, `prune`, `clear` and rename are safe.
+
+## Disk usage
+
+The cache uses about 1x the repacked tensor bytes per model and per build/patch/layout version, plus one
+header page per tensor. For Ornith that is about 9.7 GiB per version. Older versions stay until pruned.
+Suggested location: `~/.cache/local-ai/repack/`. Run `tools/repack_cache.py prune --keep-latest` (or with
+`--build/--patch`) after changing builds.
+
+## Expected effect
+
+- **Cold load** (empty cache): the old load time, plus xxh64 of the payload, plus writing about 9.7 GiB with
+  `fsync` (disk-bound, roughly 3-6 s on NVMe). One time per model and build version.
+- **Warm load**: no repack. The cost is the header checks, the `mmap`, and, with VERIFY=1 (the default),
+  one read and xxh64 pass over the cache file (about 9.7 GiB: roughly 1-2 s from the page cache, or
+  disk-bound when cold). With VERIFY=0, the pages come in lazily on first use instead. Expected to be close
+  to the pre-repack load time of about 9.6 s. Codex should measure this.
+- **RAM**: the repacked weights are file-backed (`file`/`file_mapped` in `memory.stat`, not `anon`), and
+  reclaimable. Note that page cache is still charged to the cgroup, so `memory.peak` can still include it.
+  The gain is that the kernel can reclaim it under the 28 GiB cap instead of OOM-killing.
+- **Caveat**: llama.cpp's loader maps the GGUF with `MAP_POPULATE` (prefetch on), so a warm load still reads
+  the source expert bytes of the GGUF into the page cache, even though the repack never touches them. That
+  costs I/O time and adds reclaimable page cache. Removing it would need a loader change (for example,
+  marking repack-cache tensors as lazy ranges), which is not part of this patch.
+
+## Validation steps for Codex (hardware lane)
+
+Build with the patch, then use the same command line as the existing Ornith runs, with
+`GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE=$HOME/.cache/local-ai/repack` in the environment:
+
+1. **No-cache baseline**: env var unset. Record the load time and `memory.peak`, and check that it matches
+   the current branch (the code path is unchanged apart from one branch in `set_tensor`).
+2. **Cold load**: `rm -rf ~/.cache/local-ai/repack`, then load. Expect `INFO miss` lines (90 for the Q3_K
+   experts, plus any other repacked tensors) and no `ERROR` lines. Record the load time and `memory.peak`, then
+   check `tools/repack_cache.py list` (about 9.7 GiB).
+3. **Warm load** (same binary): expect one `INFO hit` line, a summary with `misses=0 errors=0`, and no
+   `ERROR` lines. Record the load time, `memory.peak`, and `anon` vs `file` in `memory.stat` after load.
+   Optionally repeat with `GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_VERIFY=0`.
+4. **Correctness**: KLD and top-1 of the warm-cache run against the no-cache run on the usual eval
+   text. The expected KLD is exactly 0, because the bytes are identical (as the unit test checks for every
+   repacked type). Also run the protected task and check that it passes as before.
+5. **Tests**: `test-repack-cache` (single-threaded, synthetic, under 1 s).
+6. **Optional**: run two processes at once on an empty cache dir and check that both load correctly, that
+   one file remains per tensor, and that no `.tmp.` files are left.
