From 8b9f3a15662fe856490bcd0340bcd6ea5fb73bf7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oscar Nordstrom <13300531+n0rpan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 19:22:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] ggml-cpu: repack cache: ctime + explicit identity in the
 key, owner-only files

- add the model file's ctime to the key (mtime-preserving in-place rewrites)
- GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_MODEL_SHA256 / GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_ENGINE_ID go into
  the key when set; the sha replaces the head hash and source sampling
- dirs 0700, files 0600; cache files opened O_NOFOLLOW and must be regular,
  ours and not group/other-writable; unsafe roots/dirs/files fall back to
  normal packing with an ERROR line
- fix: an unusable root (or mmap failure) no longer aborts in ggml_tallocr
  because of the page alignment the buffer type already reported
- bump the default patch id to the ee6b9a1 base; tests for all of the above

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 docs/repack-cache.md               |  44 ++++++++---
 ggml/src/ggml-cpu/repack-cache.cpp | 120 +++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 ggml/src/ggml-cpu/repack-cache.h   |   2 +-
 ggml/src/ggml-cpu/repack.cpp       |   4 +-
 tests/test-repack-cache.cpp        | 112 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 tools/repack_cache.py              |  14 ++--
 6 files changed, 267 insertions(+), 29 deletions(-)

diff --git a/docs/repack-cache.md b/docs/repack-cache.md
index ac967e5..d272562 100644
--- a/docs/repack-cache.md
+++ b/docs/repack-cache.md
@@ -15,8 +15,24 @@ the repack, and the weights become clean, file-backed pages that the kernel can
 ```
 export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE=$HOME/.cache/local-ai/repack   # unset or empty: exact old behaviour
 export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_VERIFY=1                       # default; 0 skips the payload hash on hits
+# optional explicit identity from the controller (both go into the key when set):
+export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_MODEL_SHA256=<64 hex>          # replaces the 8 MiB head hash and the source sampling
+export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_ENGINE_ID=<string>             # build/source/patch fingerprint of the engine
 ```
 
+`GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_ENGINE_ID` matters because `ggml_commit()` plus a `-dirty` suffix and the default
+patch id are shared by every dirty build made from the same commit. The controller should set it to a
+fingerprint of the actual binary or source tree. A malformed `MODEL_SHA256` (not exactly 64 hex digits)
+is ignored with one `ERROR bad-model-sha256` line, and the default identity is used instead. For a split
+GGUF, the SHA should cover all parts, because it applies to every tensor.
+
+Permissions: ggml creates cache directories with mode 0700 and files with mode 0600. A cache root that is
+not owned by the current uid, or that is group- or other-writable, is rejected with
+`ERROR unsafe-cache-root`, and the load then uses normal packing. The root's parents are not checked.
+Model subdirectories must be real directories (not symlinks) with the same ownership and permission
+rules (`ERROR unsafe-cache-dir`). Cache files are opened with `O_NOFOLLOW` and must be regular files,
+owned by the current uid, and not group- or other-writable (`ERROR unsafe-file`).
+
 The directory is created when needed. It works for every repacked type (Q4_0, Q4_K, Q5_K, Q6_K,
 Q3_K, IQ4_NL, MXFP4, Q8_0, Q1_0, and the x86/RISC-V variants), because it sits in the buffer type,
 not in a per-type repack function. POSIX only (Linux tested); on Windows the variable is ignored.
@@ -25,14 +41,14 @@ Inspect and clean it with `tools/repack_cache.py`:
 
 ```
 tools/repack_cache.py list  [--dir D] [-v] [--json]
-tools/repack_cache.py prune [--dir D] [--older-than DAYS] [--build B] [--patch P] [--keep-latest] [--dry-run]
+tools/repack_cache.py prune [--dir D] [--older-than DAYS] [--build B] [--patch P] [--engine E] [--keep-latest] [--dry-run]
 tools/repack_cache.py clear [--dir D] --yes
 ```
 
 `list` groups entries by model, build and patch, with size, last use and status. `prune` always removes
-corrupt or truncated entries, entries whose model file is gone or changed (size, mtime or inode), and
-abandoned temp files older than 1 h. On request it also removes other builds or patches
-(`--build`, `--patch`), entries not used for N days (`--older-than`), and older versions of the same
+corrupt or truncated entries, entries whose model file is gone or changed (size, mtime, ctime or inode), and
+abandoned temp files older than 1 h. On request it also removes other builds, patches or engines
+(`--build`, `--patch`, `--engine`), entries not used for N days (`--older-than`), and older versions of the same
 tensor (`--keep-latest`). "Last use" is the file mtime, which ggml updates on every warm hit.
 
 ## Design
@@ -96,7 +112,8 @@ carries its hash. The key contains:
 - `format`: cache format version
 - `build`: `ggml_version()/ggml_commit()` (the commit ggml was configured from, with `-dirty` when relevant)
 - `patch`: the compile-time `GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_PATCH_ID`
-  (default `q3k-repack-1c148ab+mmid-gemm-0a3a679+repack-cache-v1`; override with `-DGGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_PATCH_ID=...`)
+  (default `q3k-repack-1c148ab+mmid-gemm-ee6b9a1+repack-cache-v2`; override with `-DGGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_PATCH_ID=...`)
+- `engine`: `GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_ENGINE_ID`, when set
 - `cpu`: feature set (neon, dotprod, i8mm, sve, sme, fp16va, avx*, amx, riscv_v, vxe, sve_cnt)
 - `repack`: the selected repack layout (block size, interleave, column count, activation type), e.g. `blk110:i8:c8:q8_K`
 - `layout`: the xxh64 of that repack function's output for a fixed synthetic 2048x16 input, computed
@@ -106,10 +123,15 @@ carries its hash. The key contains:
 - source identity, one of:
   - `src=file` (normal mmap load): the GGUF that backs the source pointer, found through `/proc/self/maps`
     and confirmed with `stat` (device, inode): `model` path, `model_dev`, `model_ino`, `model_size`,
-    `model_mtime_ns`, `model_head` (xxh64 of the first 8 MiB of the file, which covers the GGUF header,
-    the KV metadata and usually the tensor infos; computed once per file), `tensor_offset`, and `src_sample`
-    (xxh64 of 8 x 1 KiB samples of the source tensor bytes)
