--- a/docs/repack-cache.md
+++ b/docs/repack-cache.md
@@ -14,7 +14,7 @@ the repack, and the weights become clean, file-backed pages that the kernel can
 
 ```
 export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE=$HOME/.cache/local-ai/repack   # unset or empty: exact old behaviour
-export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_VERIFY=1                       # default; 0 skips the payload hash on hits
+export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_VERIFY=once                    # default; see "Verification" below
 # optional explicit identity from the controller (both go into the key when set):
 export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_MODEL_SHA256=<64 hex>          # replaces the 8 MiB head hash and the source sampling
 export GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_ENGINE_ID=<string>             # build/source/patch fingerprint of the engine
@@ -95,15 +95,45 @@ Each file is a header of one page (max(4096, page size) bytes) followed by the p
 | offset | field |
 |---|---|
 | 0 | magic `GGMLRPC1` |
-| 8 | u32 format version (1), u32 header size |
+| 8 | u32 format version (2), u32 header size |
 | 16 | u64 payload size |
 | 24 | u64 xxh64 of the payload |
 | 32 | u64 xxh64 of the key text |
 | 40 | u64 creation time (ns) |
-| 48 | u32 key length, u32 reserved |
-| 56 | full key text (`name=value` lines) |
+| 48 | u32 key length, u32 flags (bit 0: `verified`) |
+| 56 | u32 sample count (8), u32 sample size (4096) |
+| 64 | 8 x u64 xxh64 of the sampled payload windows |
+| 128 | full key text (`name=value` lines) |
 | header_size-8 | u64 xxh64 of header bytes [0, header_size-8) |
 
+The sampled windows are 8 evenly spaced 4 KiB ranges of the payload (window i starts at
+`i*(payload-4096)/7`), so the first and last 4 KiB are always included. The key contains `format=2`, so
+entries from format 1 get a different file name and are a plain miss, not an error.
+
+### Verification
+
+`GGML_CPU_REPACK_CACHE_VERIFY` selects how much of the payload is checked:
+
+| value | on write | on every hit |
+|---|---|---|
+| `once` (default, also unset/empty) | full xxh64 while writing; after `fsync`, the file is mapped and hashed again; only on a match is the header rewritten with `verified=1` and the file renamed into place | the cheap checks plus the 8 sampled windows; no full hash |
+| `1` / `full` | same as `once` | the cheap checks, the samples, and a full xxh64 of the payload (debugging) |
+| `0` / `off` | same as `once` | the cheap checks only |
+
+The cheap checks run in every mode: `O_NOFOLLOW`, a regular file, owner and permission checks, the file
+length, magic, version, header size and header hash, the payload size, and a byte-for-byte compare of the
+full key. In `once` and `full` modes, a header without `verified=1` is also rejected (`unverified`).
+An unknown value prints one `WARN bad-verify-mode` line and uses `once`.
+
+Why `once` is enough here: the cache lives on btrfs, which checksums data blocks and returns EIO
+(`read-failed`, or SIGBUS on a mapped page) on bitrot instead of returning bad bytes. The files are written
+atomically (temp file, `fsync`, verify, rename), are owner-only (0600), and are owner-checked on open. So a
+full hash on every hit would mostly re-check what btrfs already guarantees, and it costs a full read of
+about 9.7 GiB per load. The samples are still cheap (8 pages per tensor) and catch truncation-like damage
+or gross corruption, such as an overwritten or zero-filled file. Corruption of a single byte outside the
+sampled pages is only caught with `full`; the test documents that trade-off. On a filesystem without data
+checksums, consider `full`.
+
 ### Key
 
 The full key text is stored in the header and compared byte for byte on every hit. The file name only
@@ -148,7 +178,10 @@ ggml-repack-cache: ERROR <reason> key=<keyhash16> path=<file> [errno=N] [tensor=
